speechtext:
Yes, or of the ageing of the population, because every one of us has noticed in the paper every few days an item saying that some prominent man in the life of the country or some friend has suddenly died from coronary thrombosis. It is getting to be more than a disease of old age or of middle age, because the age limit seems to be coming down. I noticed the other day that a young man of thirty-I knew of him- passed out very suddenly with coronary thrombosis. Every once in a while you notice that it is getting into the lower age group;

whether it is the strain and stress of present-day life I do not know. There is something, and if we could only find the answer to that, then we would have solved another problem so far as national health is concerned.
The problem of mental disease is one which has existed for some time, and which is rapidly becoming worse. Not only are we short of accommodation for these people, but we are short of beds. Even if a certain percentage, a gradually growing percentage, of these people are cured, there are still problems regarding mental disease where research could do a great deal. We wonder why increasing numbers of the population are suffering from mental disease. We wonder whether something further could be done by way of cure. Certainly at the present time this population is growing more rapidly than we would ever like to see, and we look forward to the future still hoping that there will be no further growth in the numbers suffering from this disease. At the same time we must deal with present-day conditions as they are. I make this suggestion. Out of the national health allotment some place let us take increasing sums for research on disease.
